    "# COGS 108 - Final Project (change this to your project's title)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Permissions\n",
    "\n",
    "Place an `X` in the appropriate bracket below to specify if you would like your group's project to be made available to the public, including the names of the authors.\n",
    "\n",
    "* [  ] YES - make available\n",
    "* [  ] NO - keep private\n",
    "\n",
    "## Link to video\n",
    "https://link.to.your.publicly.viewable.video\n",
    "\n",
    "## Abstract"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The rapid increase in Generative AI adoption across all industries and individuals raises questions about the environmental sustainability. We look to investigate whether improvements in benchmark performance exhibit diminishing returns in CO2 cost. More specifically, whether pushing for increased accuracy and requires disproportionately more emissions. \n",
    "\n",
    "We use two datasets, as well as a third academic one for insights. 4465 HuggingFace models concerning inference costs and parameters, such as [co2_cost, parameters, precision, architecture, average_score], as well as benchmarks testing various tasks (BBH, GPQA, MMLU-PRO, MATH, IFEval, MUSR). 1403 Epoch AI models give us training related data, such as [Training compute (FLOP), Training chip-hours, Parameters]. In analysing the HuggingFace data, we use log-scaled co2_cost to fit a Linear model over a power-scaled X variable. For Epoch AI, we analyze log-log linear relationships between parameters and training compute. \n",
    "\n",
    "We find that Huggingface reveals parameters exhibit diminishing returns on CO2 cost (optimal b = 0.82, p < 0.001, R^2 = 0.671), marginal CO2 cost per parameter decreases as models scale. Benchmarks show weak independent association after controlling for parameters (p = 0.014, Change in R^2 < 0.001). Epoch AI confirms training compute scales faster than parameters (log-log sloppe = 1.724, R^2 = 0.766, n = 815). These datasets indicate that environmental impact is driven primarily by model scale, rather than accuracy gains, and reducing the environmental cost of Generative AI would require efficient improvements in architecture. Limitations of our findings include cross-dataset matching challenges, inference time based CO2 costs, and missingness, future analysis would look to use real energy measurements, and efficiency over model generations."
   ]
  },
