    "### Epoch AI\n",
    "\n",
    "Using the Epoch AI Models dataset, we focused on training side cost proxies, mainly Parameters and Training compute (FLOP), to describe how cost scales with model size for language domain models. Since both variables span many orders of magnitude, we worked on a log scale. For the subset of models that actually report training compute, we see a strong positive relationship between parameter count and training FLOPs, and it looks close to linear in log log space. In the log-log regression, the estimated slope is 1.724 with R² = 0.766 across n = 815 models, which helps us treat Epoch as a clean training cost axis that complements the benchmark based performance results covered elsewhere in the project.\n",
    "\n",
    "That said, Epoch has important caveats. Several compute-related fields have heavy missingness (e.g., `Training chip-hours` ≈ 90% missing and `Batch size` ≈ 86% missing), so compute-focused findings are conditional on the reported-compute subset and may be biased if reporting differs by organization, model family, or time period. Also, Parameters and FLOPs are still proxies for environmental impact rather than direct emissions, because real carbon cost depends on where training happens, the grid carbon intensity, and hardware efficiency, which are not fully captured here. Next steps would be to improve cross dataset matching between Epoch and the benchmark datasets, run sensitivity checks for how missingness changes conclusions, and incorporate more direct energy or emissions measurements when available."
